{pad:meta title='Pipes - Transform Output with Functions'/}

<p>Pipes allow you to pass output through functions to transform it. Use the pipe symbol <code>|</code> followed by a function name to process data.</p>

<h2>Two Ways to Use Pipes</h2>

<h3>1. Variable Pipes</h3>

<p>Apply functions to a variable with <code>{pad:open/}$variable | function{pad:close/}</code> - the field tag runs the sanitize chain on the result - or with <code>{pad:open/}pad:echo $variable | function/{pad:close/}</code>, which prints it raw, as the examples on this page do:</p>

{common:example 'fragments/pipes_1'/}

<h3>2. Tag Pipes</h3>

<p>Apply functions to tag output. Pipes can be placed on the opening tag or closing tag:</p>

{common:example 'fragments/pipes_2'/}

<h2>Pipe Timing: Opening vs Closing Tag</h2>

<p>The placement of the pipe determines <strong>when</strong> the function is applied:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Opening tag</strong> <code>{pad:open/}app:tag | function/{pad:close/}</code> - transforms the content template, once, before any occurrence is rendered. What the function is handed is the source between the tags, not the tag's data.</li>
  <li><strong>Closing tag</strong> <code>{pad:open/}/type:tag | function{pad:close/}</code> - transforms the output, after every occurrence has been rendered and joined.</li>
</ul>

<p>An opening pipe is therefore not a way to reorder or filter what a tag iterates: a field written inside the content would be transformed along with everything else, and then not resolve. Reshaping the data is what the <a href="?pages/data_handling">data handling</a> options are for.</p>

{common:example 'fragments/pipes_4'/}

<h2>Chaining Pipes</h2>

<p>Chain multiple pipe functions together by separating them with <code>|</code>. Functions are applied left to right:</p>

{common:example 'fragments/pipes_3'/}

<h2>Presentation helpers</h2>

<p><code>truncate</code> shortens on a whole word, <code>slug</code> makes a readable URL part,
<code>bytes</code> writes a size for people, and <code>escape</code> writes a value for the
context it lands in - <code>html</code>, <code>attr</code>, <code>js</code>, <code>css</code> or
<code>url</code>:</p>

{common:example 'fragments/pipes_5'/}

<h2>Common Pipe Functions</h2>

<table border="1" cellpadding="10" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%;">
  <tr style="background: #f0f0f0;">
    <th>Function</th>
    <th>Description</th>
    <th>Example</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><code>upper</code></td>
    <td>Convert to uppercase</td>
    <td><code>{pad:open/}pad:echo $text | upper/{pad:close/}</code></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><code>lower</code></td>
    <td>Convert to lowercase</td>
    <td><code>{pad:open/}pad:echo $text | lower/{pad:close/}</code></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><code>ucwords</code></td>
    <td>Capitalize each word</td>
    <td><code>{pad:open/}pad:echo $text | ucwords/{pad:close/}</code></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><code>trim</code></td>
    <td>Remove whitespace</td>
    <td><code>{pad:open/}pad:echo $text | trim/{pad:close/}</code></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><code>html</code></td>
    <td>HTML encode</td>
    <td><code>{pad:open/}pad:echo $text | html/{pad:close/}</code></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><code>replace(a,b)</code></td>
    <td>Replace text</td>
    <td><code>{pad:open/}pad:echo $text | replace('old','new')/{pad:close/}</code></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><code>+ n</code></td>
    <td>Add number</td>
    <td><code>{pad:open/}pad:echo $count | + 1/{pad:close/}</code></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><code>date(fmt)</code></td>
    <td>Format date</td>
    <td><code>{pad:open/}pad:echo $timestamp | date('Y-m-d')/{pad:close/}</code></td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2>Important Rules</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>A literal needs {app:tag 'echo'/}</strong> - <code>{pad:open/}pad:echo 'text' | upper/{pad:close/}</code>; a field pipes as it stands, <code>{pad:open/}$var | upper{pad:close/}</code>, as well as through <code>{pad:open/}pad:echo $var | upper/{pad:close/}</code> - which prints the value raw, without the sanitize chain the field tag runs</li>
  <li><strong>Arithmetic needs spaces</strong> - <code>{pad:open/}pad:echo $x | + 1/{pad:close/}</code> NOT <code>{pad:open/}pad:echo $x | +1/{pad:close/}</code></li>
  <li><strong>Quote string parameters</strong> - <code>{pad:open/}pad:echo $x | replace('a','b')/{pad:close/}</code></li>
  <li><strong>Functions apply left to right</strong> - <code>{pad:open/}pad:echo $x | trim | upper/{pad:close/}</code> trims first, then uppercases</li>
</ul>

<h2>When to Use Opening vs Closing Tag Pipes</h2>

<table border="1" cellpadding="10" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%;">
  <tr style="background: #f0f0f0;">
    <th>Use Case</th>
    <th>Placement</th>
    <th>Example</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Transform the content template before it repeats</td>
    <td>Opening tag</td>
    <td><code>{pad:open/}array:items | trim{pad:close/}...{pad:open/}/array:items{pad:close/}</code></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Reshape the data itself</td>
    <td>Option, not a pipe</td>
    <td><code>{pad:open/}array:items sort{pad:close/}...{pad:open/}/array:items{pad:close/}</code></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Transform final output after rendering</td>
    <td>Closing tag</td>
    <td><code>{pad:open/}type:tag{pad:close/}...{pad:open/}/type:tag | upper{pad:close/}</code></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Format variable values</td>
    <td>Variable pipe</td>
    <td><code>{pad:open/}pad:echo $price | number_format(@, 2)/{pad:close/}</code></td>
  </tr>
</table>

